1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,998
Lesson 2.1.

2
00:00:02,378 --> 00:00:05,979
Brownian motion, the Wiener
process and Itô calculus.

3
00:00:07,083 --> 00:00:12,678
In 1827 the botanist Robert Brown looked
through a microscope at pollen grains

4
00:00:12,678 --> 00:00:18,273
suspended in water: they moved ceaselessly,
zigzagging, with no visible cause.

5
00:00:18,653 --> 00:00:22,273
In 1905 Einstein explained
it: the grains are

6
00:00:22,273 --> 00:00:25,491
kicked by water molecules we cannot see.

7
00:00:25,871 --> 00:00:29,419
That motion, Brownian motion,
is the mathematical model

8
00:00:29,419 --> 00:00:32,579
of thermal noise we will
use throughout the unit.

9
00:00:33,683 --> 00:00:36,822
Let's start with a random walk: at each

10
00:00:36,822 --> 00:00:40,122
time step, the particle jumps up or down.

11
00:00:40,502 --> 00:00:45,095
Now let's make the time steps shorter
and, at the same time, the jumps smaller.

12
00:00:45,475 --> 00:00:49,359
How must the jump shrink so the
limit neither freezes nor blows up?

13
00:00:49,739 --> 00:00:54,240
The answer is that the jump must scale
as the square root of the time step.

14
00:00:54,620 --> 00:00:58,280
With that rule, the walks
converge to a continuous

15
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:01,646
but extremely rough
curve: the Wiener process.

16
00:01:02,750 --> 00:01:08,421
The Wiener process, W(t), starts at
zero, has independent increments,

17
00:01:08,801 --> 00:01:15,894
and each increment W(t) − W(s) is Gaussian
with mean zero and variance t − s.

18
00:01:16,274 --> 00:01:20,095
Look at many trajectories at
once: they fan out, and the

19
00:01:20,095 --> 00:01:23,985
width of the fan grows as the
square root of t, not as t.

20
00:01:24,365 --> 00:01:28,210
The histogram at a fixed
instant is exactly a Gaussian.

21
00:01:28,590 --> 00:01:32,697
And if we zoom into any piece of
a trajectory, it looks just as

22
00:01:32,697 --> 00:01:36,608
rough: the process is continuous
but differentiable nowhere.

23
00:01:37,717 --> 00:01:40,086
Here comes the property
that changes everything.

24
00:01:40,466 --> 00:01:43,970
Let's split the interval
from zero to T into finer and

25
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:47,474
finer pieces and add up the
squares of the increments.

26
00:01:47,854 --> 00:01:50,769
For a smooth curve that
sum would tend to zero.

27
00:01:51,149 --> 00:01:55,888
For the Wiener process it tends
to T, exactly, with no randomness.

28
00:01:56,268 --> 00:01:59,968
We summarize it with the rule (dW)² = dt.

29
00:02:00,348 --> 00:02:04,159
The increments are of order
root dt, so their squares

30
00:02:04,159 --> 00:02:07,467
are of order dt, and
they cannot be neglected.

31
00:02:08,583 --> 00:02:11,240
Consequence: the chain rule changes.

32
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:17,128
If f is a smooth function, the
Taylor expansion of f(W + dW)

33
00:02:17,128 --> 00:02:22,084
has a first-order term, f'
dW, and a second-order one,

34
00:02:22,464 --> 00:02:25,610
one half of f'' (dW)².

35
00:02:25,990 --> 00:02:31,479
In ordinary calculus the second
is dropped, but here (dW)² is dt.

36
00:02:31,859 --> 00:02:38,945
So we get Itô's lemma:
df = f' dW + ½ f'' dt.

37
00:02:39,325 --> 00:02:46,007
For example, for W², d(W²) = 2W dW + dt:

38
00:02:46,387 --> 00:02:52,111
the extra dt term is exactly what
makes the mean of W² grow as t.

39
00:02:53,217 --> 00:02:54,718
Let's put in real numbers.

40
00:02:55,098 --> 00:03:00,941
A sphere of one micrometer radius in
water at three hundred kelvin has,

41
00:03:00,941 --> 00:03:06,291
by Stokes–Einstein, a diffusion
coefficient D = k_B T / (6π η a),

42
00:03:06,671 --> 00:03:10,816
about 2.2 × 10⁻¹³ m²/s.

43
00:03:11,196 --> 00:03:16,188
Its position along one direction
is √(2D) W(t), so in one

44
00:03:16,188 --> 00:03:20,480
second it typically moves
about 0.66 micrometers.

45
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:25,254
In a hundred seconds, only ten
times more: the square root of time.

46
00:03:26,383 --> 00:03:30,595
The Wiener process is the noise
source of every stochastic

47
00:03:30,595 --> 00:03:34,735
equation in the course: the
thermal noise of circuits and

48
00:03:34,735 --> 00:03:38,075
pbits, the noise diffusion
models add to data,

49
00:03:38,455 --> 00:03:42,458
and the noise of the thermodynamic
linear solver in lab three.

50
00:03:43,583 --> 00:03:48,214
In the next lesson we will add
forces and get the Langevin equation.

51
00:03:48,594 --> 00:03:52,561
Then we will write the equation
for the probability density, the

52
00:03:52,561 --> 00:03:57,332
Fokker–Planck equation, and learn to
simulate with the Euler–Maruyama method.

53
00:03:58,450 --> 00:04:01,099
Common mistakes: scaling
the jumps like the

54
00:04:01,099 --> 00:04:03,379
time step instead of its square root.

55
00:04:03,759 --> 00:04:07,614
Applying the ordinary chain rule
and forgetting the Itô term.

56
00:04:07,994 --> 00:04:10,743
Treating white noise as
an ordinary function.

57
00:04:11,123 --> 00:04:13,387
And confusing the Itô and Stratonovich

58
00:04:13,387 --> 00:04:16,484
interpretations when the
noise depends on the state.

59
00:04:17,617 --> 00:04:20,987
In summary: the Wiener
process is the limit of

60
00:04:20,987 --> 00:04:23,918
random walks with jumps of size root dt.

61
00:04:24,298 --> 00:04:28,461
It has independent Gaussian increments
and a variance that grows as t.

62
00:04:28,841 --> 00:04:33,879
Its quadratic variation makes
(dW)² = dt, which is why Itô

63
00:04:33,879 --> 00:04:38,397
calculus has an extra term
of one half f'' times dt.

64
00:04:39,517 --> 00:04:41,868
To practice: find the distribution of an

65
00:04:41,868 --> 00:04:44,455
increment and the
covariance of the process.

66
00:04:44,835 --> 00:04:47,092
Use Itô's lemma with the exponential.

67
00:04:47,472 --> 00:04:51,030
Compute how long the sphere
takes to move ten micrometers.

68
00:04:51,410 --> 00:04:54,003
And explain why the sum
of squares converges

69
00:04:54,003 --> 00:04:56,479
while the sum of absolute values diverges.
